WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.120
From an NBA star to the King of Pop, these
famous figures turned down the chance to work

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.210
with the one and only Adam Sandler.

00:00:07.514 --> 00:00:12.920
The 1995 comedy "Billy Madison" tells the
story of dimwitted Billy, a privileged deadbeat

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.299
poised to inherit a chain of hotels from his
father, Brian.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.560
Despite Billy having graduated high school,
Brian reveals that he had bribed Billy's school

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.320
to let him pass his classes.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.770
Now, Billy must prove himself capable of running
the business by going back to school and passing

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.430
all twelve grades within six months.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.540
If he fails, the business will belong to the
hotel's evil vice president, Eric Gordon.

00:00:34.236 --> 00:00:35.780
" You're gonna give the company to Eric?!

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.133
He is a bad, bad man."

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:43.140
Initially, Sandler wanted Bob Odenkirk to
be cast as the villainous Eric, as the two

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.540
had gotten along during their time on "SNL."

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.470
But Odenkirk, far from the household name
he is today, was vetoed by studio executives.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:55.260
After an extensive series of auditions, Sandler
fell in love with the performance of an up

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.901
and coming actor named Philip Seymour Hoffman
— but executives were reluctant to hire him.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:04.640
Sandler was able to convince them to hire
Hoffman, but a new problem quickly arose — Hoffman

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.640
ended up turning down the role.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.070
In "Fly on the Wall," a podcast hosted by
David Spade and Dana Carvey, Sandler detailed

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.420
how he called up Hoffman personally to see
what was going on.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.420
He recalled —

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.690
"I go, 'Hey, I saw your tape, you're so great,
buddy, and they said you don't want to do

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:21.549
it [...] So, do you want to do it?'

00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:24.680
And he goes, 'Aww, I can't [...] I just don't
want to.'"

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.890
The role ended up being delightfully filled
by "The West Wing" star Bradley Whitford.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.020
Fortunately, Hoffman and Sandler would eventually
get the chance to work together in Paul Thomas

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.979
Anderson's romantic dramedy "Punch-Drunk Love."

00:01:35.935 --> 00:01:36.935
"Shut up!

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:38.479
Shut the f— up!"

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:39.388
"You have no right to take me—"

00:01:39.388 --> 00:01:39.979
"Shut up!

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:40.663
Will you shut up!

00:01:40.663 --> 00:01:42.104
Shut up! Shut up!

00:01:42.104 --> 00:01:44.578
Shut shut shut shut shut up!"

00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:50.340
Following the highly successful "Billy Madison"
was Adam Sandler's classic 1996 sports comedy

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.340
"Happy Gilmore."

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.790
The film chronicles the journey of the titular
failed ice hockey player Happy, who soon discovers

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.910
his talents transfer better to the world of
professional golf.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:03.990
His more rambunctious style rankles his uptight
competitor Shooter McGavin — played by Christopher

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.420
McDonald — who resorts to nefarious means
to get Gilmore off the course.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.049
McDonald's portrayal of Shooter is truly iconic,
but it almost didn't happen.

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:16.329
In an interview with Alex Withrow for And
So It Begins, McDonald confirmed that he had

00:02:16.329 --> 00:02:18.209
turned down the role twice, explaining —

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:20.749
"I was tired, I wanted to see more of my family."

00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:25.198
Fortunately, McDonald changed his mind after
winning a particularly competitive game of golf.

00:02:25.198 --> 00:02:26.101
He continued —

00:02:26.101 --> 00:02:27.428
"That high was something else.

00:02:27.428 --> 00:02:31.439
So, with my golf shoes still on, I went in
the locker room, called my agent and said,

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:35.019
'Is that golf movie still available, because
I just won this tournament and I'm feeling

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:37.438
a little bit, well, Shooterish.'"

00:02:37.438 --> 00:02:40.519
"I've worked hard my whole life, paid my dues,
and now it's Shooter's turn.

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:45.000
And Shooter's not about to let his reign at
the top be spoiled by some freak sideshow clown."

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.230
McDonald was cast, and movie magic was made.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.530
McDonald enjoyed the freedom he was given
to improvise, making sure his character didn't

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.220
just play to all of the cliched bad guy tropes.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.148
Though he initially declined the role, he
now looks back on the film as a blessing, saying —

00:02:58.148 --> 00:02:59.639
"We really had fun with that one.

00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:02.829
And the fact that that movie is still on people's
minds is great."

00:03:03.134 --> 00:03:07.099
After Christopher McDonald initially turned
down the role of Shooter McGavin in "Happy

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:10.779
Gilmore," studio executives began looking
for a possible replacement.

00:03:10.779 --> 00:03:15.190
Deciding to take a big swing, the studio reached
out to Kevin Costner, legendary star of sports

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.469
films like "Field of Dreams," "American Flyers,"
and "Bull Durham."

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:23.400
Casting Costner as a golf player sounds like
a perfect fit, and Costner himself agreed

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.269
— but he still didn't agree to the role
of Shooter.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:29.249
Instead, he was busy making his own golf movie,
"Tin Cup."

00:03:29.249 --> 00:03:35.139
"Tin Cup" earned Costner a Golden Globe nomination
for his role as Roy McAvoy, so it was probably

00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:38.269
worth it for him to swing away from "Happy
Gilmore."

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:42.510
More sports films followed for Costner, including
Sam Raimi's "For Love of the Game," the football

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.059
movie "Draft Day," and Disney's "McFarland,
USA."

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:49.959
But for a brief shining moment, the world
was nearly graced with Kevin Costner as one

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:52.430
of the funniest sports film villains ever.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.829
After finding success with "Happy Gilmore,"
Adam Sandler again returned to sports for

00:03:56.829 --> 00:03:59.870
the 1998 football comedy "The Waterboy."

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.510
Sandler stars as the titular waterboy Bobby
Boucher, who lives with his overbearing mother,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.949
played by Kathy Bates.

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:09.650
When Bobby winds up playing football instead
of just handing out drinks, he begins to make

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.229
his way out from under his mother's thumb
and into the real world.

00:04:13.229 --> 00:04:16.462
"Mama… something bad happened today."

00:04:16.462 --> 00:04:18.410
"Somebody hurt you, my boy?

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.410
Who hurt you?

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.520
You tell Mama who hurt you."

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:25.360
The Academy Award-winning Bates almost didn't
play the iconic role of Mama Boucher, as her

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.300
agent didn't like the script.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.340
Bates told SBNation —

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.620
"They made an offer, so legally, she had to
send me the script.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.340
And I read the first 12 pages, and it was
about football, and it was kind of silly,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.160
so I tossed it in the trash next to my bed."

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.760
Fortunately, Bates' niece saw the script and
recognized Adam Sandler's name.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.640
She convinced Bates to give the script another
chance — a decision that Bates now knows

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:46.940
was the right one to make.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.940
She reminisced —

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.630
"There's many experiences I've had that have
been more difficult or unpleasant or didn't

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.760
turn out the way I wanted them to turn out.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.537
This was one of the happy experiences that
I've had."

00:04:56.719 --> 00:05:04.320
In 2010, "Grown Ups" became Sandler's highest-grossing
film to date, raking in over $270 million worldwide.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.710
The movie did so well that Sandler generously
gifted brand new Maseratis to his co-stars

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:14.170
Chris Rock, Kevin James, and David Spade — as
well as a Tesla Roadster to Rob Schneider.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:19.530
After the smashing success of the first film,
a sequel followed in 2013 with one major difference:

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:24.590
Rob Schneider was absent, replaced by comedian
Nick Swardson as Schneider's character's brother.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.880
When questioned about his absence in a radio
interview, Schneider initially made it sound

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.620
like there was bad blood between him and Sandler
because of pay disputes.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.252
He said —

00:05:32.252 --> 00:05:33.892
"They're doing 'Grown Ups 2' without me.

00:05:33.892 --> 00:05:34.564
Mistake.

00:05:34.564 --> 00:05:36.501
They should have paid me a lot of money."

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:37.920
However, Schneider also added —

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.170
"Well, truthfully, I wasn't sure if I'd have
my TV series, so it was an availability thing,

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.540
but at the end of the day, they should have
[said], 'What money does Rob need?'"

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:50.220
If there really was a falling out behind the
scenes, the two seem to have made up.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.210
Schneider has gone on to appear in more recent
Sandler films, including "The Ridiculous 6,"

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.210
"Sandy Wexler," and "Hubie Halloween."

00:05:56.701 --> 00:06:03.140
The 2015 action comedy "Pixels" sees Adam
Sandler as Sam Brenner, a former arcade champion

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:07.120
recruited by the government to stop aliens
that are taking the forms of classic arcade

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.490
games like "Pac-Man," "Centipede," and "Donkey
Kong."

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:14.750
In addition to the gaming icons, the aliens
take on the appearances of famous '80s faces

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:19.060
like Ronald Reagan and Max Headroom to deliver
their messages to mankind.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:24.490
According to a report from Showbiz 411, the
infamous 2014 Sony email leak revealed that

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:29.250
the studio reached out to the estate of Michael
Jackson for a potential cameo in "Pixels."

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:33.340
While the estate didn't exactly turn them
down outright, they did request a fee that

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.870
surpassed their budget by quite a large amount.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.270
Although Sandler really wanted the King of
Pop to appear in the sci-fi adventure, the

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:43.007
Michael Jackson alien did not moonwalk its
way into the final cut.

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:48.030
Adam Sandler's first of many Netflix films
was the Western parody "The Ridiculous 6."

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.780
The film stars Sandler as a man raised by
Native Americans who learns he has a handful

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:56.980
of eccentric half-brothers — played by Terry
Crews, Jorge Garcia, Taylor Lautner, Rob Schneider,

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.050
and Luke Wilson.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.680
The brothers team up to rescue their newfound,
recently kidnapped father by stealing enough

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.235
money to pay his ransom.

00:07:03.235 --> 00:07:07.404
"Any of you handsome brothers ready to go
steal a big hunk of gold?"

00:07:07.404 --> 00:07:08.280
"I'm ready."

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.280
"I'm hungry."

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.034
"I'm drunk."

00:07:10.034 --> 00:07:11.034
"And I'm Lil' Pete!"

00:07:11.034 --> 00:07:15.880
Despite its eclectic cast, critics and audiences
alike were less than pleased with the movie.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.730
Many criticized the film as racially insensitive,
with loads of unfunny jokes that relied on

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.200
terrible stereotypes.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.960
But it wasn't just the audience saying so
— this criticism also came from some of

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.050
the movie's own cast members.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:31.230
As reported by Indian Country Today, many
of the Native American extras were upset at

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.940
the lowbrow jokes and inaccurate costumes.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:38.080
This led to about a dozen actors and crew
members, including actor Loren Anthony and

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.870
the Native American cultural advisor, to leave
the set in protest.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.310
Netflix responded to the controversy by telling
Vulture —

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:48.520
"The movie has ridiculous in the title for
a reason: because it is ridiculous.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:53.130
It is a broad satire of Western movies and
the stereotypes they popularized, featuring

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:56.669
a diverse cast that is not only part of — but
in on — the joke."

00:07:56.669 --> 00:08:00.280
Perhaps the film's low approval rating proves
that Sandler should have listened to what

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.104
his coworkers had to say.

00:08:02.104 --> 00:08:08.880
The 2022 Netflix sports drama "Hustle" stars
Adam Sandler as down-on-his-luck NBA scout Stanley Sugerman.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.230
When he's sent to Spain to recruit a rising
star, Sugerman stumbles across an even better

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:17.170
player named Bo Cruz, played by Juancho Hernangómez.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:21.620
Unable to convince his boss of Cruz's talent,
Sugerman quits and focuses on preparing Cruz

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.060
for the NBA Draft.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:27.451
Hernangómez is an NBA star who's previously
played for a number of teams, currently for

00:08:27.451 --> 00:08:28.710
the Toronto Raptors.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:33.030
"Hustle" was his acting debut, but he was
initially reluctant to take the role.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.519
In an interview with Pop Culture, Hernangómez
stated —

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:36.519
"I told them no.

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:37.969
I mean, I didn't want to do it.

00:08:37.969 --> 00:08:41.310
I mean, I don't want to waste time in something
like this.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.310
It's not my dream."

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:46.329
Eventually, Hernangómez was convinced by
his sister to take the role, which he later

00:08:46.329 --> 00:08:48.529
described as a great experience, saying —

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:51.629
"I was really surprised how hard they work
for making a movie.

00:08:51.629 --> 00:08:52.999
I just enjoy the journey so much.

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:55.709
I mean, I embrace it and I enjoy it."

00:08:55.709 --> 00:08:59.639
Hernangómez had a blast working with Sandler,
feeling an instant connection with the comedy

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:01.019
legend on set.

00:09:01.019 --> 00:09:02.019
He continued —

00:09:02.019 --> 00:09:04.089
"We enjoy it so much every single day.

00:09:04.089 --> 00:09:05.439
We spent two months together.

00:09:05.439 --> 00:09:08.899
We didn't have any bad days [...] I feel like
that's the reason Bo and Stanley have that

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:12.092
good chemistry because in real life, we got
a great feeling."

00:09:12.092 --> 00:09:16.649
While Adam Sandler has acted in several dramatic
films, none have had quite the same impact

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:23.234
as his portrayal of the gambling-addicted
jewelry salesman Howard Ratner in 2019's "Uncut Gems."

00:09:23.234 --> 00:09:26.759
Critics heaped praise on his performance,
and many believed the film would earn Sandler

00:09:26.759 --> 00:09:28.950
his first Academy Award nomination.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:33.720
While the Oscar evaded the Sandman, he still
managed to nab best actor wins from the Independent

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:36.029
Spirit Awards and the National Board of Review.

00:09:36.438 --> 00:09:37.910
"This is my f—--- way.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.533
This is how I win.

00:09:39.533 --> 00:09:40.334
Alright?"

00:09:40.334 --> 00:09:43.100
Of course, he almost didn't take the role
at all.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:48.370
Filmmakers Josh and Benny Safdie initially
reached out to Sandler's management in 2015,

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.440
but were told he was unavailable.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:54.730
The brothers made attempts with other actors,
including "Borat" comedian Sacha Baron Cohen

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.310
and "21 Jump Street" star Jonah Hill.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:02.300
After struggling to de-age the character,
Hill eventually left to work on other projects.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:06.319
With more projects now under their belt, including
the critically acclaimed Robert Pattinson-led

00:10:06.319 --> 00:10:10.990
thriller "Good Time," the Safdie brothers
decided to reach out to Sandler one last time.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.149
Luckily, this time he agreed to the role,
ushering in a whole new era of critical respect

00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:17.470
and acclaim for the famously goofy actor.

00:10:17.733 --> 00:10:22.740
The animated "Hotel Transylvania" franchise
has made over a billion dollars worldwide.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.970
Adam Sandler lent his voice to the first three
movies as none other than Count Dracula, who

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:31.980
owns the titular hotel for monsters and struggles
to be a good father to his daughter Mavis.

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:36.290
The films also feature a number of Sandler's
frequent live-action collaborators, including

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.089
Andy Samberg, Kevin James, Steve Buscemi,
and David Spade.

00:10:40.089 --> 00:10:45.040
When the fourth film, "Hotel Transylvania:
Transformania," premiered in 2022 on Amazon

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:49.910
Prime, most of the actors reprised their monstrous
roles — with two major exceptions.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:54.319
First off, Frankenstein was no longer played
by Kevin James, but instead by voice actor

00:10:54.319 --> 00:10:55.579
Brad Abrell.

00:10:55.579 --> 00:10:59.949
More surprisingly, though, the lead role of
Dracula was now occupied by Adam Sandler impressionist

00:10:59.949 --> 00:11:00.949
Brian Hull.

00:11:00.949 --> 00:11:04.420
Director Derek Drymon spoke of the casting
change, telling Screen Rant —

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:08.810
"The fact that he turns into a human was a
good opportunity to do things a little differently.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.829
He could be a little different than he was
in the movies, and it would be natural.

00:11:11.829 --> 00:11:16.010
So, it kind of was the perfect movie to have
a person come in and fill those shoes."

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:20.180
Though there is no official reason behind
Sandler's lack of involvement on the project,

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:23.629
some have theorized that it had to do with
his Netflix contract, and another animated

00:11:23.629 --> 00:11:26.513
film he was developing with the competing
streaming service.

